\description{
Fits the false-positive occupancy model of \code{\link[=fp_occu]{fp_occu()}} to several species
at once, with the coefficients of each species drawn from distributions
of the community, so that rare species borrow strength from the others.
\strong{This function is in development} (see \link{crywolf-api}): its arguments,
results and output names may change in a minor version. Besides \code{print()},
\code{summary()} and \code{as_draws_df()}, it works with \code{\link[=fp_sites]{fp_sites()}} (by site and
species), \code{\link[=fp_ppc]{fp_ppc()}}, \link[=simulate.fp_occu_ms]{simulate()} and
\code{\link[=fp_richness]{fp_richness()}}; the other methods of \code{\link[=fp_occu]{fp_occu()}} are not available.
}
\details{
Each species \eqn{s} has its own occupancy coefficients
\eqn{\beta_s = \mu_\beta + \sigma_\beta z_s} (all the terms of \code{occ}, with
\eqn{z_s} standard normal), its own true-detection intercept
\eqn{\alpha_s = \alpha + \sigma_\alpha z_s}, and its own gap between the
true-detection and false-positive intercepts,
\eqn{g_s = \mu_g \exp(\sigma_g z_s)}, which keeps the false-positive rate
of every species below its true-detection rate at the reference. By
default the slopes of \code{det}, \code{fp} and \code{ver}, the intercept of the verified
method and the recorder effect are shared by the species; \code{by_species}
gives each species its own slopes of \code{det} or \code{fp}, or its own intercept of
the verified method, drawn around the community value as the intercepts
are (\eqn{\alpha_{sk} = \alpha_k + \sigma_{\alpha k} z_{sk}}). The false positives of a
species are independent of those of the others: the classifier errs in
labelling a species, not in confusing two species.

Two random effects let the false-positive rate vary beyond its
covariates, on its logit, both with the half-Normal prior of
\code{fp_priors(fp_site_sd = )}: \code{fp_group} names a column of the sites that
groups them (for example the physical site, when each "site" of the data
is a site in one month), and adds an effect for each group and species,
\eqn{v_{gs} \sim N(0, \sigma_v)}; \code{random_fp = TRUE} adds an effect for
each site and species, \eqn{t_{is} \sim N(0, \sigma_t)}. With sites that
are site-months, \code{fp_group} lets the false-positive rate of a species
differ among physical sites, and \code{random_fp} among months of the same
site as well.

The species are those of the data (\code{fp_data(species = )}), and a species
is never assumed to be absent from the list: there is no data
augmentation for species never detected.

\strong{Output names.} The community means have the names of \code{\link[=fp_occu]{fp_occu()}}
(\verb{occ_*}, \verb{det_*}, \verb{fp_*}, \code{fp_gap}, the median gap, and \code{p11_ref},
\code{p10_ref} at the community means); the standard deviations among species
are \verb{sd_occ_*}, \code{sd_det_(Intercept)} and \code{sd_log_fp_gap}; and the values
of each species carry its name in brackets: \verb{occ_*[species]},
\code{det_(Intercept)[species]}, \code{fp_gap[species]}, \code{p11_ref[species]} and
\code{p10_ref[species]}. With \code{by_species}, the slopes add \verb{sd_det_*},
\verb{det_*[species]}, \verb{sd_fp_*} and \verb{fp_*[species]}, and the verified method
adds \code{sd_verified_(Intercept)}, \code{verified_(Intercept)[species]} and
\code{p_verified[species]}. The standard deviations of the random effects on
the false-positive rate are \code{sd_fp_group} (\code{fp_group}) and \code{sd_fp_site}
(\code{random_fp}).
}
